**Describe the bug**
When passing a descriptor instance via a constructor and assigning it to an attribute of a class defining `__slots__` the type of the class attribute is then narrowed to the type of the passed descriptor. This incorrectly makes `pyright` infer that accessing the attribute will invoke `Descriptor.__get__`.

```python
from typing import Any, Literal, Self, assert_type, overload


class Base:
    pass


class Descriptor[T]:

    @overload
    def __get__(
        self, obj: Literal[None], objtype: type[Base] | None = None
    ) -> Self: ...

    @overload
    def __get__(self, obj: Base, objtype: type[Base] | None = None) -> T: ...

    def __get__(
        self, obj: Base | None, objtype: type[Base] | None = None
    ) -> Self | T:
        if obj is None:
            return self
        else:
            return obj.__dict__[self.name]

    def __set__(self, obj: Base, value: T):
        obj.__dict__[self.name] = value

    def __set_name__(self, owner: Base, name: str):
        self.name = name


class ObjectNoSlots:

    def __init__(self, descriptor: Descriptor[Any]):
        self.descriptor = descriptor


class ObjectWithSlots:

    __slots__ = "descriptor"

    def __init__(self, descriptor: Descriptor[Any]):
        self.descriptor = descriptor


class Foo(Base):

    valid = Descriptor[bool]()


# This works fine, without slots "descriptor" is not a descriptor
# at class level, and the type of it is not narrowed by assignment
# in the constructor
no_slots = ObjectNoSlots(descriptor=Foo.valid)
print(no_slots.descriptor.name)
assert_type(no_slots.descriptor, Descriptor[Any])


# This fails, it seems the assignment of descriptor inside the
# constructor narrows the type to Descriptor[Any] and this causes
# attribute access to assume that ObjectWithSlots.descriptor is
# now of type Descriptor[Any]
slots = ObjectWithSlots(descriptor=Foo.valid)
print(slots.descriptor.name)
assert_type(ObjectWithSlots.descriptor, Any)
assert_type(slots.descriptor,

**Describe the bug**

Inconsistent behaviour when assigning a valid dictionary to a variable hinted as a TypedDict with `total=False` if `issubclass` is used.

**Code or Screenshots**

This is okay.

```python
    def __init_subclass__(cls, test: bool) -> None:
        cls.kwargs: SentinelKwargs = SentinelKwargs(init=False) if test else {}
```

This is also okay.

```python
    def __init_subclass__(cls, test: bool) -> None:
        cls.kwargs: SentinelKwargs = {}
```

This is not okay.

```python
    def __init_subclass__(cls) -> None:
        cls.kwargs: SentinelKwargs = SentinelKwargs(init=False) if issubclass(cls, MappedAsDataclass) else {}
```

This is also not okay.

```python
class TestClass: ...

class SentinelMixin:
    def __init_subclass__(cls) -> None:
        cls.kwargs: SentinelKwargs = SentinelKwargs(init=False) if issubclass(cls, TestClass) else {}
```

