**Describe the bug**

Per PEP 544 [here](https://peps.python.org/pep-0544/#merging-and-extending-protocols):

> Subclassing a protocol class would not turn the subclass into a protocol unless it also has typing.Protocol as an explicit base class. **Without this base, the class is “downgraded” to a regular ABC that cannot be used with structural subtyping.**

It is understandable that Pyright would emit an error whenever a plain class inherits from a `Protocol` but does not implement all required methods. However, it is less convincing, especially in light of the above language in the PEP, that an `ABC` inheriting from `Protocol` should also have this requirement.

It doesn't seem good to follow the PEP 544 language to its most liberal conclusion (any class that incompletely implements a protocol should be considered an ABC automatically). However, in the event that a class is marked explicitly as `ABC`, it seems reasonable?

**Notably, the error below _can be avoided_ by re-declaring the abstract methods.** This feels very odd to do, and is extremely unwieldy for e.g. a mixin for a `Protocol` with many methods. It would also imply that an added method on the `Protocol` should require _every mixin for that Protocol to be updated with no change to behavior_, which is much less than ideal.

(This follows up on https://github.com/microsoft/pyright/issues/3730)

